WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.280 --> 00:05.720
So what is essential?

00:05.960 --> 00:09.600
It's going to return true if the value is null.

00:09.640 --> 00:10.920
So it is checking the value.

00:10.920 --> 00:12.720
If it's null it's going to return true.

00:13.000 --> 00:15.320
Otherwise it's going to return false.

00:15.360 --> 00:16.440
Now the exact opposite.

00:16.440 --> 00:18.840
If you go use the is not null.

00:18.840 --> 00:24.080
So if you use these keywords it's going to returns a true if the value is not null.

00:24.120 --> 00:27.520
Otherwise if it is null, it's going to go and return a false okay.

00:27.600 --> 00:29.480
So the syntax for that is very simple.

00:29.480 --> 00:32.000
It starts with a value or expression.

00:32.200 --> 00:36.080
And then after that we're going to have the keyword is space null.

00:36.120 --> 00:38.240
And there is not is exactly the same.

00:38.240 --> 00:39.360
So we have a value.

00:39.400 --> 00:42.320
Then afterwards we have that is not null.

00:42.320 --> 00:44.400
So we have the not operator after that.

00:44.440 --> 00:46.240
And the is not is exactly the same.

00:46.240 --> 00:47.480
So we have a value.

00:47.520 --> 00:51.280
Then we have the is not the not operator then the null.

00:51.280 --> 00:52.200
So it's very simple.

00:52.200 --> 00:53.320
Let's have an example.

00:53.320 --> 00:56.600
We are checking whether the values of the shipping address is null.

00:56.600 --> 00:59.720
So we can have it like this shipping address is null.

00:59.760 --> 01:02.280
Or we can check the opposite whether it's not null.

01:02.280 --> 01:04.440
So the shipping address is not null.

01:04.560 --> 01:05.520
It's very easy Okay.

01:05.560 --> 01:07.400
So now let's understand how this works.

01:07.520 --> 01:08.760
We are checking the value.

01:08.760 --> 01:12.560
So if the value is null then return it true.

01:12.800 --> 01:15.240
If it is not null then we return a false.

01:15.240 --> 01:18.880
So as you can see it never returns the value itself or any nulls.

01:18.880 --> 01:22.320
So we are getting a boolean of true and false.

01:22.320 --> 01:26.480
So we are creating like a boolean flag in order to assist us with the checks.

01:26.480 --> 01:27.840
So we have this very simple example.

01:27.880 --> 01:30.280
Price is null and we have those two rows.

01:30.280 --> 01:32.480
So we are checking whether the price is null.

01:32.520 --> 01:35.520
In the fourth order it is not null right.

01:35.560 --> 01:37.960
That's why we will get a false in the output.

01:37.960 --> 01:40.640
And the second order the value is null.

01:40.800 --> 01:42.440
So it is correct.

01:42.440 --> 01:43.840
That's why we will get true.

01:43.840 --> 01:48.560
Now of course if we go and use the is not null it's going to be exact opposite.

01:48.560 --> 01:50.960
So is the price not null.

01:50.960 --> 01:52.320
Well yes it's not null.

01:52.320 --> 01:54.960
That's why you will get a true over here.

01:54.960 --> 01:58.000
So now for the second check it is null right.

01:58.000 --> 02:00.320
So the output is going to be false.

02:00.640 --> 02:02.320
We will get the exact opposite.

02:02.320 --> 02:02.920
So that's it.

02:02.920 --> 02:05.960
It's very simple how the is null and is not null works.

02:09.830 --> 02:10.350
All right.

02:10.350 --> 02:16.750
One very obvious use case for Isnull and is not null is by searching for missing informations or searching

02:16.750 --> 02:17.510
for nulls.

